{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"assignee":"Joe Oakhart","description":"## Context / Problem\nAn LLM plan review can consume the full model cost, return `PASS`, and still leave the reviewed ticket without a signed plan-review attestation unless signing was separately requested. That discards the review's durable gate artifact: the subsequent claim path cannot distinguish the paid, passing review from no review at all and may require another billable run or a manual recovery step.\n\nThe intended contract is already stated by the dedicated `rebar review-plan` documentation and library docstring: a non-blocking PASS signs the plan-review attestation consumed by the claim gate. All public routes that perform the same billable plan review must converge on one default: sign a fresh attestation after PASS. Deliberately skipping the signature must be explicit, visible, and exceptional.\n\n**Scope decision (2026-07-24): MCP is OUT of scope for this epic.** The MCP `review_plan`\ntool derives signing from server read-only mode (`sign=not ro`, `src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py`) and\nexposes no caller-facing `sign` parameter. Adding one — plus the machine-readable read-only\nunsigned reason — is a distinct public-contract change to a different boundary, with its own\ngenerated-reference and tool-schema consequences. It is split out rather than carried here.\nThis epic now covers the **CLI** and **library** plan-review boundaries only.\n\n## Reproduction Steps\n1. Create or select an open, non-exempt ticket with a reviewable plan and no current plan-review attestation.\n2. Run the plan-review operation through a public plan-review route without an explicit no-sign option.\n3. Allow the LLM review to complete with verdict `PASS`.\n4. Run `rebar verify-signature <ticket-id> --output json`.\n5. Observe that verification reports the ticket as unsigned even though the full review cost was incurred and no opt-out was requested.\n\n## Expected Behavior\nEvery mutating public plan-review entry point signs and persists a plan-review attestation by default when the final verdict is a signable, non-degraded `PASS`. The attestation binds the reviewed material fingerprint and verified source SHA exactly as the claim gate expects.\n\nSkipping signing requires an explicit opt-out at the relevant boundary: `--no-sign` for CLI use, `sign=False` for library use. The output clearly states whether the attestation was signed and, when deliberately skipped, why.\n\n## Actual Behavior\nThe default behavior is correct on the CLI and library routes, but nothing at the real parser/API boundary PINS it: a future refactor could silently flip the default with no test failing. The signing default is therefore an undefended invariant rather than a defect in itself.\n\n## Approach\n1. Inventory the CLI dispatcher, the dedicated `review-plan` command, and the library `review_plan` call paths and identify where the default changes or the signing wrapper is bypassed.\n2. Route every mutating plan-review surface through the same sign-on-PASS finalization seam. Keep the structural guards that prohibit signatures for BLOCK, INDETERMINATE, degraded, local/unattested-source, and otherwise non-signable results.\n3. Make deliberate unsigned execution explicit at each public boundary. CLI exposes `--no-sign`; the library exposes `sign=False`.\n4. Preserve idempotent reuse of an already-current attestation. A default invocation should neither rerun the LLM nor emit a duplicate signature when the claim gate already accepts the current attestation.\n5. Preserve signing-failure behavior: a PASS whose signature persistence was attempted but failed is not reported as ordinary success. It returns the established retryable failure disposition and points to the non-LLM `sign-review` recovery path when the PASS sidecar remains current.\n6. Update user, CLI, and LLM-framework documentation so the default, opt-out syntax, non-signable verdicts, and recovery behavior match the implementation.\n\nRejected: leaving signing opt-in to avoid ticket writes. The review is already a gate operation whose purpose is to produce the claim-consumable attestation; callers needing a pure read have an explicit no-sign mechanism. Rejected: signing BLOCK, INDETERMINATE, degraded, or local-source results, because those are not certifiable PASS outcomes. Rejected (2026-07-24): carrying the MCP `sign` opt-out here — see the scope decision above.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/_cli/_llm_commands.py`\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/__init__.py`\n- `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/attest.py`\n- plan-review dispatcher/finalization modules found during the call-path inventory\n- `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_plan_review_gate.py`\n- `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_exit_codes.py`\n- library surface tests for plan review\n- `docs/cli-reference.md`\n- `docs/user-guide.md`\n- `docs/llm-framework.md`\n- `docs/plan-review-gate.md`\n\nExplicitly NOT in scope: `src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py`, MCP surface tests, and generated MCP\nreference inputs/outputs.\n\n## Testing / Verification\n- Unit tests cover sign-on-PASS and never-sign verdict/source guards at the shared finalization seam.\n- CLI tests exercise default signing and explicit `--no-sign` through the actual argument parser and dispatcher.\n- Library contract tests exercise default `sign=True` and explicit `sign=False`.\n- An end-to-end lifecycle test runs a passing plan review through the real CLI route, verifies the resulting signature, and then proves the claim gate accepts it without a second LLM call.\n- Documentation/help drift checks pin the default and every supported opt-out/recovery path.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A non-exempt ticket receiving a non-degraded PASS through each mutating public plan-review entry point (CLI `review-plan`, library `review_plan`) gets a persisted plan-review attestation by default; interface tests verify the attestation is present and claim-valid.\n- [ ] An end-to-end lifecycle test invokes the real CLI `review-plan` route without an opt-out, verifies the attestation matches the current material fingerprint, and proves a subsequent `rebar claim` succeeds without another LLM call.\n- [ ] CLI unsigned execution requires the explicit `--no-sign` flag; parser tests prove omission signs and presence deliberately skips signing.\n- [ ] Library unsigned execution requires explicit `sign=False`; contract tests prove the default is `sign=True`.\n- [ ] BLOCK, INDETERMINATE, degraded, exempt, and unattested local-source results remain unsigned; table-driven tests verify each guard.\n- [ ] A PASS with an attempted signature-persistence failure returns the established retryable nonzero disposition and identifies `sign-review` as the recovery path; `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_exit_codes.py` verifies the behavior.\n- [ ] Re-running the default operation with a current claim-valid attestation reuses it without an LLM call or duplicate signature event; an interface test verifies both conditions.\n- [ ] `rebar review-plan --help` states that PASS signs by default and names the explicit unsigned exception; a help drift test verifies the generated text.\n- [ ] `docs/cli-reference.md`, `docs/user-guide.md`, `docs/llm-framework.md`, and `docs/plan-review-gate.md` accurately describe sign-on-PASS, explicit opt-out, never-sign outcomes, and `sign-review` recovery; documentation checks pass.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the default non-integration pytest suite exit zero.\n"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1784935487186415001,"uuid":"ca252ff1-fe14-482a-827e-6c2bc100d258"}